arguments.content:
⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `_openUrl` can throw past the bridge — the JS promise never settles**

`canLaunchUrl` and `launchUrl` both can throw (`PlatformException`, e.g. Android `ActivityNotFoundException`), but `_openUrl` awaits them bare. An exception here escapes into the engine's handler dispatch, so the JS-side `jsr.openUrl()` promise is left pending forever instead of rejecting with `{'__error': ...}` — and the error may trip the runtime's uncaught-async-handler path.

Every neighboring bridge handler in this file guards against exactly this (e.g. the fetch handler wraps its body in `try/catch` and resolves `{'__error': error.toString()}`). Match that pattern:

```dart
Future<void> _openUrl(String id, String url) async {
  try {
    final uri = Uri.tryParse(url);
    if (uri == null || !await canLaunchUrl(uri)) {
      _resolve?.call(id, {'__error': 'cannot launch $url'});
      return;
    }
    final ok = await launchUrl(uri, mode: LaunchMode.externalApplication);
    _resolve?.call(id, ok ? true : {'__error': 'launch failed'});
  } catch (e) {
    _resolve?.call(id, {'__error': 'cannot launch $url: $e'});
  }
}
```

